Use several different tools and resources to confirm and clarify God’s calling to plant a church.

Description
Missional Church planting is one of the most demanding and faith-stretching things you can do. It is also one of the most rewarding and satisfying. But, it is not for everyone.
Take time to study and learn about yourself and see how your gifts and strengths can be used to start a healthy church.
Also, submit yourself to the review and opinions of church planting experts to confirm that you are qualified and prepared to plant a church.
Most importantly, spend lots of time in prayer seeking God’s confirmation of the calling.','group',1,null,null,true,false,true,3,'','[]'::jsonb);
Understand the importance of being called as a missionary to plant a church and how to affirm that calling from God.

Description
Before starting a church, you should have a clear calling from God.
Part of understanding a missional calling to plant a church is hearing the calling affirmed from other mature Christians. One of the best affirming processes available is a church planter assessment. In the post https://app.asana.com/app/asana/-/get_asset?asset_id=1202503599965551 by Dave Earley, the first 2 success factors are:
    calling, and
    assessment
Assessment has become an ''industry'' standard. For the RAM Network we have each potential church planting missionary go through several types of assessments.

Actions
    Talk to mature Christians whom you respect. Get their wisdom and ask their feedback on your calling to plant a church.
    Talk to peers in ministry and ask their feedback on your calling to plant a church.
    Read https://churchplants.com/articles/6081-15-ways-to-know-if-youre-a-church-planter.html by Hal Seed.
This action is complete after you have finished watching the training video.','task',6,null,null,true,false,true,3,'','[]'::jsonb);
Identify the approximate geographic area for the new church so you can begin proper planning.

Description
God''s call for you to plant a church will include being called to a people and/or a place. In the early stages of the plant, you may not have clarity on exactly where to plant, but you need to begin discerning roughly where God is calling you if you''re to make any plans. Your approximate geographic area might be a region, a county, or a metro area.
In some cases, the area is chosen by a sending church or church planting network before they even call you to plant with them. In other cases, you may identify the area collaboratively after you''ve been called by the church planting network.
The location you settle on should be informed by prayer and initial demographic profiles/studies. Here are things to consider in selecting a location:
    The demographics - Who lives in the area? Who will you be reaching in the area?
    Population density - How many people of your target demographic live within a reasonable driving distance?
    Who you are - You will attract others like yourself.
    Other churches - What other healthy, growing churches exist in the area?
    Growth patterns - Over the past 5 years, has the area been growing or declining? What are the projections for the next 5-10 years?
    Other new churches - Are other church planting networks targeting the same area? Most church planting networks have a priority list of locations.
    God’s calling - Is The Spirit leading you to a specific place?
    Setting - are your skills and experiences a better match for an urban, suburban, or rural setting?
 You will refine your target area in a subsequent PlanterPlan action.

Actions
    Seek God’s counsel for the general area the church is to be planted.
    Review and consider the factors listed above.
    Select a general area to target for starting the new church.
This action is complete once you have prayerfully selected the general area for the church plant.

