Complete an assessment by an independent organization that specializes in discerning a leader’s suitability for planting.

Description
Placing enthusiastic people in posts for which they are unprepared or unqualified is extremely counterproductive. It often ends up even damaging them and their families.
Assessment is the process of discerning two important factors in church planting:
    Qualification
    Readiness
We''re all wired up differently. Certain personality types do really well as lead planters. Some types are a bad match and attempting to plant will cause untold anxiety, stress and even emotional damage.
And even those that have the natural temperament to plant may not have the life and ministry experience necessary to start a new church.
A formal assessment will let you know where you stand on both of these counts. The assessment can be as short as one day, but is commonly 3-4 days in length. It''s often done in a group setting with other potential planters and includes a combination of:
    interviews
    group exercises
    essays
    assignments
The evaluation team observes you in a number of different situations.
You will usually get a detailed report and an individual formal debrief. The report will include your strengths as well as developmental suggestions. It will make a clear recommendation as to your readiness to plant a church within the next 2 years.
Assessment History
For two decades, assessment has been used by church planting strategists across North America. Borrowing from experts like Dr. Thomas Graham and Dr. Charles Ridley, a process has been developed to help interested candidates determine their giftedness and readiness for church planting, either as the leader of a team, a member of a team, or as a ‘solo’ church planting pastor. Church planting assessment integrates several lenses of evaluation to help a couple or individual discover as much about healthy placement in church planting as possible. These lenses include:
    Written expression, in the form of an extensive questionnaire, a professional resume, exercises, and projects, are evaluated by the assessment staff
    Psychological instruments that are employed to identify any potential issues which impact individual wholeness, marital adjustment, and professional interaction
    Experienced practitioners observing candidates in simulation modules, team activities, and personal interviews to provide real-life perspective for potential church planting scenarios
    Peer assessments that provide each candidate with invaluable input from others with whom he/she works during the assessment
    Self assessment - perhaps the most important directional outcome of all. Processing all input available, the candidate (and spouse where applicable) is able to make a highly informed conclusion about placement and timing in church planting ministry.

Actions
    https://form.jotform.com/212844457592161
    Arrange for travel and childcare as appropriate.
    Participate in the event.
This action is complete when the planter has undergone a formal assessment.','task',9,null,null,true,false,true,3,'','[]'::jsonb);
Understand why having a coach should be a non-negotiable.

Description
Coaching has become a ''best practice'' of church planting and is required by the majority of church planting networks and denominations. In fact, according to the Leadership Network, planters who have a coach are twice as likely to plant a healthy church that survives.
As in double.
Coaches offer an experienced perspective on the unique tasks and experiences you''ll go through. Better than just providing sage advice, your coach will help bring out the best in your thoughts and planning process: coaching is not telling people what to do and how to do it. Coaches are trained to listen and not impose their experience on you.
Coaches also hold you accountable. When a planter has committed to making 10 networking appointments by the end of the week, does he really want to call his coach and admit that he didn’t do it? We all need this kind of motivation for time to time.
Coaches can also be your confidante. You can tell your coach anything. You can share honest thoughts, feelings, and emotions about what is happening in the new church. People who will do that for you are rare in the life of any planter, but so very needed.
After experiencing the support of a coaching relationship, many leaders will continue to intentionally seek out a coach for the rest of their time in ministry.
Christian Coaching
Here are some basic assumptions of Christian coaching:
    People have unique visions and values
    People have a need to discover individually what God wants them to do
    The Holy Spirit provides the Christian with personal guidance
    Each person has a unique background, knowledge, experience, insight and setting
    Christians need to lead according to a unique calling from God
    If you discover it yourself, you own it better
    A coach is not the expert on your life and won''t tell you what to do
    People are more committed to what they help create
    God leads each of us uniquely to unique paths
    Prayer is our most powerful tool
A Tale of Two Coaches
Andre had to pick between launching worship gatherings in February at a decent location, or waiting until April to launch in a fantastic location. He didn''t know how to choose between buying more time before the first summer and waiting for the better location. Through conversations with his coach, he was able to determine the best choice for his church. Without the counsel of someone who has ‘been there and done that’, Andre wouldn''t have had anyone with experience to help weigh the options.
Chris had a different problem: people he invited to join the Launch Team kept telling him, “Let us know when you’ve started weekly services, then we’ll come be a part of what you’re doing.” His frustration grew and he couldn’t figure out how to gain traction. To save money, he had chosen not to hire a coach, but he really needed someone with experience to process this landmine and figure out how to diffuse it.
You may be tempted to save money and not hire a coach. Don''t cut corners on this one! Make hiring a coach a non-negotiable for yourself.

Actions
