This action is complete when you have viewed the training video.','task',10,null,null,true,false,true,3,'','[]'::jsonb);
Have you ever noticed that the best players in sports all have coaches? Doesn''t it seem like players of great caliber wouldn''t need a coach?
Whether in sports, business, or church planting, everyone needs a coach. In sports, the coach''s primary role is to bring out the best in the athlete, and in team sports to get all the players working together.
In church planting these two goals still apply: bringing out the best in you, the church planter, and getting you working with God. The coach accomplishes these goals through asking the right questions and challenging you to think God-thoughts and make Godly decisions. This can include:
    Training and empowering you
    Bring you wisdom, experience and external perspective
    Being your cheerleader
    Helping you see your blind spots
    Helping you navigate the stress of being an entrepreneur with the demands of of a healthy family life
    Providing a safe place for you to have doubts and ask questions
    Asking you the tough questions
Qualifications
To be effective, the coach should have personally planted a successful church or have other similar personal experience with church planting. Since you probably haven''t planted a church before (usually), the coach’s experience is a great way for the church planter to learn from someone else, both the good and the bad.
Choosing the right coach is critical. Sometimes church planting networks train and select coaches for the church planter. Other times you choose your own coach. Regardless, you need a coach you respect both personally and professionally. And you need to share fundamental beliefs.
As an absolute minimum, the coach should be available for a monthly conference call. A more ideal arrangement is a biweekly 1-hour phone call with the church planter. The coach should commit to working with the Church Planter for 1-2 years. Whenever possible, you should be involved in choosing your coach.
Gain Wisdom
Building a church is complicated. It requires a great deal of broad knowledge that few church planters possess.
Instruct a wise man and he will be wiser still; teach a righteous man and he will add to his learning. - Proverbs 9:9
The way of a fool seems right to him, but a wise man listens to advice. - Proverbs 12:15
We all need someone to bounce ideas off. Get a coach.

Actions
    Request a coach from the Church Planting Director
    Fill out the Coach/Planter Covenant

