Set up a recurring calendar appointment to facilitate meeting with your coach on a regular basis.

Description
Sometimes the hardest part of developing a coaching relationship is simply beginning the relationship. Once you''ve selected a coach, take the lead to contact the coach and set up an initial meeting. This meeting will probably happen by phone or video call. If you can swing it, meet with the coach in person the first time around.
The first meeting should open a dialog about what both parties hope to gain from the relationship. It is also important at this time to set expectations on what a typical meeting will entail. Spend some time casting vision about the new church for the coach, too.
The most important thing to accomplish at the first meeting is to set up the ongoing rhythm of calls. It''s common for that to be biweekly or semi-monthly, but planting a church is a fast-moving target, so Passion for Planting recommends meeting no less than once a month with your coach.

Actions
    Set initial meeting agenda.
    Conduct initial meeting.
    At that meeting, schedule ongoing meetings.
    Add the new schedule to your calendar as a recurring appointment.
This action is complete when regular meetings have been scheduled with the coach.','task',12,null,null,true,false,true,3,'','[]'::jsonb);
Train the coach on the use of Asana Tasks so that coaching calls can focus on the big issues that you face.

Description
The coach should review the project status via Asana prior to any coaching calls or meetings to understand the current status of the church plant. This will enable the coach to focus on the most important issues facing the planter.
Integrated into Asana are scheduled coaching calls and coaching checkpoints. The coaching call tasks equip the coach with questions to ask that are pertinent to the planter within the timeline of the launch plan. The coaching checkpoints allow the coach to provide input and affirmation of critical strategy documents the planter creates.
The coach may also see trends in the planter’s work patterns and determine adjustments need to be made.

Actions
    Grant the coach access to your Asana project by clicking The red plus button at the top of the screen.
